/* data/learn-c/phy-12-13.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-12-13.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-13"] = {"13.1":{"s":"DC has a fixed polarity. AC changes direction many times a second (50 times a second in India). AC is easy to step up and down with transformers, so it is used for power supply. This chapter studies how resistors, inductors and capacitors behave with AC.","x":["Direct current (DC) flows one way only. Alternating current (AC) changes its size and its direction again and again, following a sine curve. The mains supply in India is AC at 50 Hz. In each second the current reverses its direction 100 times.","AC is used for power supply because its voltage can be raised or lowered easily with a transformer. High voltage means small current, so less energy is lost as heat in the long power lines. At home the voltage is brought down to 230 V.","This chapter shows how a resistor, an inductor and a capacitor each behave when the current alternates. Then it joins them in a series circuit and studies power, resonance and the choke coil.","Common mistake: using the DC rules directly. In AC, the inductor and the capacitor give an opposition that depends on the frequency, and the voltage and current may not reach their peaks together."],"e":"A mains supply has a frequency of 50 Hz. Its period is 1/50 = 0.02 s. In one period, the current flows once in each direction, so it is zero 100 times in one second."},"13.2":{"s":"An AC generator gives e = e₀ sin ωt, where e₀ is the peak emf and ω is the angular frequency. The current in a circuit connected to it also varies like a sine wave.","f":[{"x":"e = e_0*sin(omega*t)","n":"Emf of an AC generator; e_0 = peak value, omega = angular frequency.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.2, p.288"},{"x":"i = i_0*sin(omega*t + alpha)","n":"Current in the circuit; alpha = phase difference from the emf.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.2, p.288"}],"x":["An AC generator turns a coil in a magnetic field. The flux through the coil changes as it turns, and by Faraday's law an emf is induced. For a coil turning at a steady rate, the emf is e = e₀ sin ωt. Here e₀ is the peak emf and ω is the angular frequency, ω = 2πf.","If the circuit connected to the generator is a simple one, the current has the same shape: i = i₀ sin(ωt + α). The angle α is the phase difference between the current and the emf. It is zero for a resistor, and it is not zero for a coil or a capacitor.","The emf is largest when the coil is moving across the field lines, and zero when the coil is moving along them. The peak emf is e₀ = NBAω for a coil with N turns of area A.","Common mistake: forgetting that ω is in radians per second. If the frequency is given in Hz, first write ω = 2πf."],"e":"A generator gives e = 325 sin 314t volts. Then e₀ = 325 V, ω = 314 rad/s, and f = ω/2π = 314/6.28 = 50 Hz. At t = 0, the emf is zero."},"13.3":{"s":"The average of AC over a full cycle is zero, so we use other values. The RMS (root mean square) value gives the same heating as that much steady DC: I(rms) = I₀/√2 ≈ 0.707 I₀. Meters show RMS values. 230 V mains is the RMS value; the peak is about 325 V.","f":[{"x":"i_av = (2/pi)*i_0 = 0.637*i_0","n":"Average over HALF a cycle (over a full cycle it is zero).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.3, p.289"},{"x":"i_rms = i_0/sqrt(2) = 0.707*i_0","n":"rms value; the same for the emf: e_rms = e_0/sqrt(2).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.3, p.289"}],"w":["AC meters read rms values; the peak is √2 times larger.","The average of AC over a full cycle is zero; the 0.637 value is for a half cycle."],"x":["The average of an alternating current over a full cycle is zero, because the positive half cancels the negative half. So it is not a useful measure. The average over half a cycle is 0.637 times the peak value, which is 2/π times the peak.","The heating effect does not depend on the direction of the current, so we use the root mean square (rms) value. It is the square root of the average of i². For a sine wave, the average of sin² is 1/2, so I_rms = I₀/√2 = 0.707 I₀. In the same way, V_rms = V₀/√2.","A steady DC current I_rms gives the same heat in a resistor as the AC with the peak value I₀. This is how the rms value is defined. AC meters and the ratings of home devices give rms values.","Common mistake: using the peak value in P = I²R. The average power is I_rms² R, which is half of I₀² R."],"e":"The mains voltage is 230 V rms. Its peak is 230 × 1.414 = 325 V. A 10 Ω heater on this supply has I_rms = 23 A and power 230²/10 = 5290 W."},"13.4":{"s":"A phasor is a rotating arrow whose length is the peak value and whose angle shows the phase. Drawing voltage and current as phasors turns AC problems into simple vector diagrams. The angle between the two phasors is the phase difference.","w":["Phasors turn anticlockwise; a phasor ahead in the turning direction leads."],"x":["A phasor is an arrow drawn for an AC quantity. Its length is the peak value, and it rotates anticlockwise at the angular speed ω. The part of the arrow along the vertical axis at any moment is the value of the quantity at that moment, V₀ sin(ωt).","Two phasors that rotate together keep a fixed angle between them. This angle is the phase difference. A phasor that is ahead in the direction of rotation leads, and the one behind lags.","Phasors are added like vectors. This is the way to add voltages across different parts of an AC circuit, because they are not in phase and their peak values cannot simply be added. For a series circuit, the same current flows in every part, so the current phasor is drawn first.","Common mistake: adding the peak or rms voltages across R, L and C as plain numbers. Always add them as arrows with the right directions."],"e":"Voltage phasors of 30 V (along the current) and 40 V (90° ahead of the current) are added as perpendicular arrows. The resultant is √(30² + 40²) = 50 V, not 70 V."},"13.5":{"s":"In a resistor, voltage and current are in phase. In an inductor, current lags voltage by 90°, and the opposition is X(L) = ωL. In a capacitor, current leads voltage by 90°, and X(C) = 1/(ωC). In a series LCR circuit the total opposition is the impedance Z = √(R² + (X(L) − X(C))²).","f":[{"x":"X_L = omega*L = 2*pi*f*L","n":"Inductive reactance (ohm); current lags the voltage by 90 degrees.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.5, p.292"},{"x":"X_C = 1/(omega*C) = 1/(2*pi*f*C)","n":"Capacitive reactance (ohm); current leads the voltage by 90 degrees.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.5, p.294"},{"x":"Z = sqrt(R^2 + (X_L - X_C)^2)","n":"Impedance of a series LCR circuit.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.5, p.295"},{"x":"tan(phi) = (X_L - X_C)/R","n":"Phase angle by which the voltage leads the current.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.5, p.296"}],"w":["In DC (f = 0) an inductor has zero reactance and a capacitor infinite reactance: a capacitor blocks DC.","Add the voltages across R, L and C as phasors, not as plain numbers."],"x":["Resistor: the voltage and the current are in phase, and the opposition is R. Inductor: the current lags the voltage by 90°, and the opposition is the inductive reactance X_L = ωL = 2πfL. Capacitor: the current leads the voltage by 90°, and the opposition is the capacitive reactance X_C = 1/ωC = 1/(2πfC). Reactance is measured in ohm.","At zero frequency (DC), X_L is zero and X_C is infinite, so a capacitor blocks DC. At high frequency X_L is large and X_C is small. So an inductor passes low frequencies and a capacitor passes high ones.","In a series LCR circuit, the voltages across L and C are in opposite directions on the phasor diagram. So the impedance is Z = √(R² + (X_L − X_C)²). The phase angle is given by tan φ = (X_L − X_C)/R. If X_L is greater than X_C, the voltage leads the current. If X_C is greater, the current leads.","Common mistake: writing Z = R + X_L + X_C. Reactances are not added to R as plain numbers."],"e":"A series circuit has R = 40 Ω, X_L = 50 Ω and X_C = 20 Ω. Z = √(1600 + 900) = 50 Ω. tan φ = 30/40 = 0.75, so φ is about 37°, and the voltage leads the current. At 200 V rms, the current is 200/50 = 4 A."},"13.6":{"s":"Average AC power is P = V(rms) I(rms) cos φ, where cos φ is the power factor and φ is the phase angle. A pure inductor or capacitor takes no average power, and the current through it is called wattless current. A pure resistor has power factor 1.","f":[{"x":"P_av = e_rms*i_rms*cos(phi)","n":"Average (true) power; cos(phi) = power factor.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.6, p.298"},{"x":"cos(phi) = R/Z","n":"Power factor of a series LCR circuit; 1 for a pure resistor, 0 for pure L or C.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.6, p.298"}],"w":["A pure inductor or capacitor stores and returns energy each cycle: average power zero (wattless current)."],"x":["At each moment the power in an AC circuit is v × i. This changes with time. The useful number is the average power over a cycle: P = V_rms I_rms cos φ. The quantity cos φ is the power factor, where φ is the phase angle between the voltage and the current.","For a series circuit, cos φ = R/Z. This gives P = I_rms² R. All the average power is spent in the resistance. For a pure resistor, cos φ = 1, and P = V_rms I_rms. For a pure inductor or a pure capacitor, φ = 90°, cos φ = 0, and P = 0.","In a pure inductor or capacitor, the energy goes from the source and is stored in a quarter of the cycle, then it returns in the next quarter. The current is called wattless current, because it does no net work, although it is not zero.","Common mistake: using P = V_rms I_rms for a circuit that has L or C. A low power factor means that the supply must carry a larger current for the same useful power."],"e":"A circuit takes 4 A at 200 V, with a power factor of 0.8. P = 200 × 4 × 0.8 = 640 W. The same power from a circuit with a power factor of 1 would need only 3.2 A."},"13.7":{"s":"A charged capacitor connected to an inductor makes energy swing back and forth between the electric field and the magnetic field. These are LC oscillations, with natural frequency f = 1/(2π√(LC)). With no resistance they would go on for ever.","f":[{"x":"f = 1/(2*pi*sqrt(L*C))","n":"Natural frequency of LC oscillations.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.7, p.300"}],"w":["Real LC oscillations die out: the coil's resistance turns energy into heat and some is radiated."],"x":["Charge a capacitor, and then connect it across an inductor. The capacitor discharges through the coil, and the current builds up. The coil resists the change, so when the capacitor is empty, the current is at its largest, and it keeps flowing and charges the capacitor in the opposite way. The charge and current swing back and forth. These are LC oscillations.","Energy moves between two forms. When the charge is largest, all the energy is in the electric field of the capacitor, q²/2C. When the current is largest, all the energy is in the magnetic field of the coil, LI²/2. The total stays the same if there is no resistance.","The frequency of the oscillations is f = 1/(2π√(LC)), and ω = 1/√(LC). It is the same as the frequency of a mass on a spring, with L like the mass and 1/C like the spring constant.","Common mistake: thinking they go on for ever. In a real circuit, the resistance turns energy into heat and some energy is radiated, so the oscillations die out."],"e":"A circuit has L = 2 mH and C = 5 μF. LC = 10⁻⁸ s², so √(LC) = 10⁻⁴ s. f = 1/(2π × 10⁻⁴) = 1592 Hz. If C is made 4 times larger, the frequency halves, to about 796 Hz."},"13.8":{"s":"In a series LCR circuit, the current is largest when X(L) = X(C). This happens at the resonant frequency ω = 1/√(LC). There, the impedance is just R and voltage and current are in phase. A radio is tuned to a station this way.","f":[{"x":"f_r = 1/(2*pi*sqrt(L*C))","n":"Resonant frequency (X_L = X_C), for both series and parallel circuits.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.8, p.301"}],"w":["Series resonance: impedance least, current largest (acceptor). Parallel resonance: impedance largest, current least (rejector)."],"x":["In a series LCR circuit with a source of fixed voltage but changing frequency, the current changes with the frequency. At low frequency X_C is large, and at high frequency X_L is large. In between, there is a frequency at which X_L = X_C. This is resonance.","At resonance, the two reactances cancel. So the impedance is the least possible, Z = R, and the current is the greatest, I = V/R. The current is in phase with the voltage, and the power factor is 1. The resonant frequency is ω₀ = 1/√(LC), or f₀ = 1/(2π√(LC)).","The voltages across L and C are equal in size and opposite in direction, so they cancel in the total. But each can be much larger than the source voltage. A radio set uses this: the tuning capacitor is changed until the circuit resonates with the wanted station.","A series circuit accepts its resonant frequency, with the least impedance. A parallel LC circuit does the opposite. At resonance its impedance is the largest, and it draws the least current from the source. So it rejects that frequency."],"e":"A circuit has R = 20 Ω, L = 0.10 H and C = 10 μF. ω₀ = 1/√(0.1 × 10⁻⁵) = 1000 rad/s, f₀ = 159 Hz. At resonance, X_L = X_C = 100 Ω, Z = 20 Ω. With a 100 V source, the current is 5 A."},"13.9":{"s":"The Q factor tells how sharp the resonance peak is: Q = ω(r)L/R. A high Q (small R) gives a tall, narrow peak, so the circuit picks one frequency and rejects its neighbours. That is good for tuning.","f":[{"x":"Q = omega_r/(omega_2 - omega_1)","n":"Q factor: resonant frequency over bandwidth; omega_1, omega_2 = where the current is 1/sqrt(2) of its peak (half power).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.9, p.303"}],"w":["Smaller R gives a higher Q and a sharper, more selective resonance."],"x":["A resonance curve is a graph of the current against the frequency. It has a peak at ω₀. Some circuits give a tall narrow peak, and some give a low wide one. The sharpness of the peak is measured by the quality factor Q.","Q = ω₀L/R = (1/R)√(L/C). It can also be written as Q = ω₀/(ω₂ − ω₁). Here ω₁ and ω₂ are the two frequencies at which the current falls to 1/√2 of its peak value, and ω₂ − ω₁ is the bandwidth. For a series circuit, the bandwidth is R/L.","A smaller resistance gives a larger Q. The peak is then taller and narrower, so the circuit picks out one frequency and rejects the nearby ones. This is what is wanted in a radio tuner. A large resistance gives a flat curve and poor selection.","Common mistake: thinking that a large Q means a large current at all frequencies. It means a large current only close to resonance."],"e":"A circuit has R = 20 Ω, L = 0.10 H and ω₀ = 1000 rad/s. Q = 1000 × 0.10/20 = 5. The bandwidth is R/L = 200 rad/s, and ω₀/bandwidth = 1000/200 = 5, the same answer."},"13.10":{"s":"A choke coil is an inductor with very little resistance, used to limit AC current. Because its power factor is almost zero, it reduces the current while wasting almost no energy as heat, unlike a resistor. Tube-light chokes are an example.","f":[{"x":"cos(phi) = R/sqrt(R^2 + omega^2*L^2)","n":"Power factor of a choke; nearly zero because R is very small compared with omega L.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 13.10, p.303"}],"w":["A choke cuts the current with almost no heat loss; a resistor would waste energy as I²R."],"x":["A choke coil is a coil of many turns with a large inductance and a very small resistance, often wound on an iron core. It is used to reduce the current in an AC circuit, for example in a tube light.","Suppose you want to cut an AC current to a smaller value. A resistor would do it, but it turns the lost energy into heat, I²R. A choke limits the current by its reactance X_L = ωL, and it takes almost no average power. Its power factor, cos φ = R/√(R² + ω²L²), is nearly zero because R is very small.","The current through an ideal choke lags the voltage by 90°, so the energy is stored and returned in each cycle. In a real choke, a small amount of energy is lost in the wire and in the iron core.","A choke works only for AC. For DC the reactance is zero, so it does not limit a steady current. Only the small resistance of its wire does."],"e":"A lamp works at 30 V and 0.5 A, and the supply is 230 V. A series resistor must drop about 200 V at 0.5 A, which wastes about 100 W as heat. A choke of a suitable inductance can limit the current to 0.5 A with almost no heat."}};
